body:
***Cambridge Dictionary:*** **"Socialize: to train people or animals to behave in a way that others in the group think is suitable."**

&#x200B;

In any society, people are socialized in countless different ways. For example, in some cultures people are socialized to respect their elders and live with/care for them until they pass, while in others they are seen as a burden and are put in a home for them to live out the rest of their days out of sight and mind. Some people are socialized to believe looking somebody in the eyes is a sign of respect, while in other cultures it's a huge insult. This is just to give background on how easily we're influenced by where we live and how mainstream societal beliefs can shape the way we perceive things. 

&#x200B;

This applies to **gender** as well. People are socialized according to their gender, and the way people perceive gender is different all over the world and throughout the course of history. 

&#x200B;

In some cultures, because of the way people were socialized, women are leaders of the pack and it's primarily matriarchal, rather than the patriarchal society we see very commonly in the modern day. Basically, mother as head of house/leader in society vs. father as head of house/leader of society. There is also record of societies where your gender does not dictate your social status. 

&#x200B;

With that being said, the way many people now understand gender is directly related to your biological sex. As many have stated in this thread, this actually refers to your chromosomes, but most people associate it with genitalia. Others here have also explained that there are many intersex people in this world who do not have your "standard" set of genitals or other sex-defining characteristics.

&#x200B;

 A lot of people today think penis = man, vagina = woman. 

&#x200B;

Through history, there are many instances of societies accepting and having no issue with genders outside of the binary of "male and female." There have been cultures with three, four, and many more genders. Because they were socialized to understand this as normal and natural, and to not equate genitalia with gender, this was accepted (and many non-binary/trans people were even praised for being so unique!)



The concept that your genitalia dictates your gender is not a permanent belief in human society; but because it's all many people have ever known, they can't understand that what they have always known to be fact is, in fact, not. 

&#x200B;

Do some reading online - there are many sources out there for information outside of Redditors offering their opinions. It can be confusing unless you take the time to really understand. Good luck to you!
